UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 1: Multiplication and division word problems', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total (×) versus sharing/grouping (÷).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose × or ÷: (a) 7 rows of 4 chairs (b) 35 stickers shared among 5 children.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7.\n\nWATCH FOR\nKeyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSolve ten mixed ×/÷ word problems. For each, name structure (groups/array/sharing/grouping), write the expression and a sentence answer. Include two remainder stories.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total (×) versus sharing/grouping (÷).","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-MR-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Solve ten mixed ×/÷ word problems. For each, name structure (groups/array/sharing/grouping), write the expression and a sentence answer. Include two remainder stories. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for word problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation.. Correction uses (a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7.. Why: restores accurate word problem.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses word problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with word problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for word problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about word problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for word problem. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7","Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Choose × or ÷: (a) 7 rows of 4 chairs (b) 35 stickers shared among 5 children. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for word problem; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of multiply and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total","(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named multiply and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","Name multiply and explain why it matters only, without linking it to word problem","Apply word problem to new material and name the multiply and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies word problem with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught word problem method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7","Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","Read 7 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 7 and 4 as interchangeable"],"question":"Using the method for word problem, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a word problem problem with numbers such as 7 and 4, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same word problem method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 7 and 4 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation"],"question":"Solve a fresh word problem problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7","Label units when comparing 7 with 4","Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation","Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for word problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses word problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for word problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about word problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with word problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation.. Correction uses (a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7.. Why: restores accurate word problem.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w12-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise word problem.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use word problem with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify multiply and explain why it matters.","I can practise divide independently on a new prompt.","I can notice choose needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total (×) versus sharing/grouping (÷).","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEqual groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared among 6\" is ÷.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose × or ÷: (a) 7 rows of 4 chairs (b) 35 stickers shared among 5 children.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7.","explain":"Multiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total (×) versus sharing/grouping (÷).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Story structures for × versus ÷.","caption":"Read structure, not only keywords.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Multiply</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">8 packs of 6</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Divide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">48 shared among 6</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Equal groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared am Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Equal groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared am Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEqual groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared among 6\" is ÷. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Array total contrasted with equal sharing.","caption":"Name the structure before the expression.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rows</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">7×4 chairs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Share</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">35÷5 stickers</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nKeyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation. Here is a more productive model: Equal groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared among 6\" is ÷.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) 7×4=28 (b) 35÷5=7.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Keyword hunting without structure reading often picks the wrong operation. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMultiplication and division word problems require choosing the operation from the story structure: equal groups total (×) versus sharing/grouping (÷). Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 1: Multiplication and division word problems","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Multiplication and division","week":12,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nEqual groups unknown total → multiply. Known total shared into equal groups → divide. \"8 packs of 6\" is ×; \"48 shared among 6\" is ÷.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Story structures for × versus ÷.","caption":"Read structure, not only keywords.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Multiply</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">8 packs of 6</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Divide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">48 shared among 6</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Array total contrasted with equal sharing.","caption":"Name the structure before the expression.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rows</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">7×4 chairs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Share</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">35÷5 stickers</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-mat-y3-w12-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
